WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Before you touch a single tool, you need to understand what HVAC systems
actually do.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:40.000
In this lab, you will physically identify every major component on a residential
split-system air conditioner. You will locate the compressor, condenser coil,
fan motor, capacitor, contactor, and service valves on the outdoor unit. On the

00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Discharge Line: The hot gas line from the compressor outlet to the condenser
coil inlet. Hot to the touch during operation.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Liquid Line: The smaller copper line carrying warm high-pressure liquid from the
condenser to the metering device.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Suction Line: The larger insulated copper line carrying cold low-pressure vapor
from the evaporator back to the compressor.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Here's why this matters on the job: Component identification is the foundation
of every service call. If you cannot find the capacitor in 30 seconds, you
cannot diagnose a no-cooling call efficiently.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Watch out for these: The capacitor stores electrical charge even when power is
off — always discharge it before handling. And — The contactor may be stuck
closed (welded contacts) — check before assuming the system is off

00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Take a minute before you move on. What's the one thing from this lesson you need
to remember on a service call?

